// @run
// @log 1
// A BARE FIELD READ off an unbounded `<T>` — the other route to the same rung, and the
// one with no UFCS step in front of it (D1004). The parameter is an entirely open hole
// (owner ruling, 2026-09-03; `docs/constraints-design.md` §7.7), so `x.n` derives its
// type from the read and each call decides whether its argument carries the field.
//
// The bounded sibling is `error-strict-body-member-not-in-bound.vl`: a bound is a
// PROMISE the body is held to (OQ-5, strict), and declaring one is still what makes the
// body's own errors precise. What changed is that not declaring one is no longer a
// refusal — `<T: { n: i32 }>` remains legal and remains stricter.
function getN<T>(x: T): i32 {
  x.n
}

print(getN({ n: 1 }))
